WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.453
Browser storage and client state are not
trusted security boundaries.

2
00:00:06.533 --> 00:00:12.883
localStorage, sessionStorage, IndexedDB,
query strings, hidden fields,

3
00:00:12.963 --> 00:00:18.321
and client-side component state can
support useful workflows,

4
00:00:18.401 --> 00:00:24.652
but they are not the right place for
secrets or authoritative permissions.

5
00:00:24.732 --> 00:00:29.197
The browser is an environment controlled
by the user.

6
00:00:29.277 --> 00:00:34.238
Client state can improve performance and
user experience.

7
00:00:34.318 --> 00:00:41.164
It can remember view preferences, draft
choices, non-sensitive workflow hints,

8
00:00:41.244 --> 00:00:46.800
or cached data that the application is
comfortable revalidating.

9
00:00:46.880 --> 00:00:52.039
It should not decide who owns a record,
which tenant is active,

10
00:00:52.119 --> 00:00:57.279
what role a user has, or whether a
sensitive action is allowed.

11
00:00:57.359 --> 00:01:02.022
Protected browser storage has important
limitations.

12
00:01:02.102 --> 00:01:05.178
It can help with specific scenarios,

13
00:01:05.258 --> 00:01:10.913
but it should not be treated as a
universal production secret store.

14
00:01:10.993 --> 00:01:15.558
Teams should understand what protection it
provides,

15
00:01:15.638 --> 00:01:20.301
which hosting models it supports, how keys
are handled,

16
00:01:20.381 --> 00:01:25.639
and what data remains visible or usable in
the browser context.

17
00:01:25.719 --> 00:01:32.566
JavaScript interop crosses a boundary
between .NET code and browser JavaScript.

18
00:01:32.646 --> 00:01:36.714
Data passed through interop should be
reviewed,

19
00:01:36.794 --> 00:01:42.548
especially when it includes
user-controlled values, identifiers,

20
00:01:42.628 --> 00:01:47.688
sensitive fields, or values used by
third-party libraries.

21
00:01:47.768 --> 00:01:54.515
Interop can be necessary and useful, but
it deserves clear ownership and review.

22
00:01:54.595 --> 00:02:01.838
Third-party JavaScript, browser
extensions, analytics scripts, embedded content,

23
00:02:01.918 --> 00:02:06.383
and component libraries can change the
risk picture.

24
00:02:06.463 --> 00:02:11.325
They may observe page behavior, manipulate
the document,

25
00:02:11.405 --> 00:02:16.267
load additional resources, or change how
data is handled.

26
00:02:16.347 --> 00:02:23.590
A production Blazor application should
know which browser-side dependencies it uses

27
00:02:23.670 --> 00:02:24.364
and why.

28
00:02:24.444 --> 00:02:28.611
Authoritative checks belong on the server
or API.

29
00:02:28.691 --> 00:02:34.248
The client may hold temporary state and
coordinate the interface,

30
00:02:34.328 --> 00:02:40.479
but the server should confirm identity,
authorization, resource scope,

31
00:02:40.559 --> 00:02:47.009
and business rules before returning
protected data or performing important

32
00:02:47.089 --> 00:02:48.180
operations.
